Methodologies become portable cognitive machinery.

Once you strip a methodology away from the domain that originally produced it, what remains is usually a sequence of operators:

Methodology → procedure → operators → invariants → transferable capability.

That’s the interesting transition. “Scientific method,” “design thinking,” “mission planning,” “literary criticism,” “debugging,” even a martial art can stop being merely ways of doing a particular thing and become temporary programs for organizing perception and action.

In Pantheon terms, methodologies can become:

* Lenses when they change what you notice.
* Roles when you temporarily think according to their discipline.
* Operators when individual moves can be extracted and recombined.
* Compilers when they translate messy inputs into a standardized reasoning process.
* Interfaces when they expose otherwise inaccessible structure.
* Daemons when the method becomes sufficiently formalized to run repeatedly on its own.
* Meta-methods when you can compare methodologies, extract their common machinery, and synthesize new ones.

And then there’s another recursion:

Methodologies become objects that methodology can operate on.

You can Indigo-Hammer the scientific method, apply the scientific method to mission planning, use literary analysis on debugging logs, apply adversarial red-teaming to your own introspection, etc.

At that point the library is no longer a collection of things you know how to do.

It becomes a library of executable ways of knowing.

Which is suspiciously close to what we’ve been building.